Declarations are private to their module by default. In `@mod.moth` and
`@package.moth`, place `$export` before a declaration to include it in that
facade's public API. Add one `@route` operand to publish it under a public route
instead.

### Exporting declarations

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- @mod.moth
    private_helper || -> String {
        return "private"
    }

    $export
    public_name #= "Moth"

    $export
    render || -> String {
        return private_helper()
    }
]

`public_name` and `render` are visible through the module's normal facade. `private_helper` remains private. An unmarked facade exports nothing.

`$export` applies to the next eligible top-level item only. It takes no operand
or one bare `@route` path after its name. It may share a line with other source
directives, such as `$doc [: Public form.] $export @forms`, because only the
target must begin on a later line. Comment-only lines may intervene, but a blank
line breaks attachment. It is valid only in the two facade files, including when
both exist in one directory. It does not apply in `@entry.moth`, ordinary helper
files or the build-only `@config.moth`, which has no export surface.

The directive can mark a function, struct, choice, transparent type alias,
trait or compile-time `#` constant. The `#` marker controls when a constant is
evaluated, not whether it is public.

Runtime bindings, mutable bindings, executable statements, templates, page
fragments, trait conformances and incompatibilities cannot be exported.
Receiver methods are exposed through their public receiver type, not exported
independently. A routed receiver type carries its methods for callers.

### Public routes

A route publishes an exported name under a namespace below the facade. The
facade root stays the default:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- child/@mod.moth
    $export
    root_value #= "root"

    $export @forms
    button #|label String| -> String {
        return label
    }

    $export @forms
    badge #|label String| -> String {
        return [:badge:[label]]
    }

    $export @forms/validation
    validate |value String| -> String {
        return value
    }
]

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    @child root_value
    @child/forms button as primary_button, badge
    @child/forms/validation validate as validate_form
]

`button` and `badge` merge into the `forms` route. Distinct names may share a
route. `validate` sits in the nested `forms/validation` route and does not
appear in `forms`. A consumer names each public route below the facade. A bare
`@child` clause exposes only root members, so it reaches `root_value` but not
`button`.

A route names where a binding is published, never what it selects or how the
leaf is spelled. The route operand belongs to the declaring facade and resolves
from that facade. It is not inherited from a provider. If `child` re-exports
`@provider/nested widget` under `$export @mirror`, the public path is
`@child/mirror`, not `@child/nested`. Aliases still decide the public leaf:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- library/@mod.moth
    $export @forms
    @shapes Shape as Form, wrap_message as shape_label
]

`@library/forms Form` and `@library/forms shape_label` are public. The aliases
are the only local and public names: `Shape` and `wrap_message` are not bound in
this facade. Select either original spelling separately when it must cross too.

Route operands use dependency-path spelling. A route is a suffix under its
facade: `@/` is rejected as a public root (`MOTH-SYNTAX-0021`), a bare `@` fails
as an empty path (`MOTH-SYNTAX-0018`), and `.` or `..` steps and file extensions
are invalid. The compiler also rejects an extra token and a malformed operand.
One target has one public destination, so two `$export` directives on that
target are a duplicate (`MOTH-SYNTAX-0021`) even when their routes differ, and
the diagnostic labels both directives:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- Error: one target has one public destination
    $export @forms
    $export @tables
    value #= 1
]

A route never publishes a whole namespace, a namespace alias or a wildcard.
Only declarations and flat direct selections cross the facade.

### Route identity and conflicts

The public identity of a routed export is `(route, leaf)`. Repeated `$export`
directives may add distinct leaves to one route. A duplicate leaf in the same
route is `MOTH-RULE-0081`, reported at both authored sites.

Any two distinct route spellings in one facade must differ by more than ASCII
case. `@forms` and `@Forms` conflict with `MOTH-RULE-0092` at both route
operands, including when the conflict appears deeper in a multi-segment route.

A routed re-export still binds its public leaf as a local name in the facade.
Publishing one selected target under two routes keeps its one local leaf and
works. A direct declaration that shares a local leaf with a routed re-export
collides before publication. Two different provider targets that both use the
same leaf also collide (`MOTH-IMPORT-0009`), even when their routes differ. A
facade therefore can't put two distinct declarations with the same leaf name in
different routes.

Concrete route overlaps are rejected eagerly, even when no consumer references
the route. A route that overlaps a different visible provider, such as a
selectable child facade or one of its routes, a local source or child namespace
key, or a registered source or binding package prefix, reports
`MOTH-IMPORT-0006`. A case-only match counts as a collision. The diagnostic
names both authored sites when both exist.

Two facades that share one visible key create an ambiguous base. The base and
every route beneath it are ambiguous. Identical route claims from different
facades behind that base stay latent and report `MOTH-IMPORT-0006` when a
consumer names them, like the base itself. A route behind an ambiguous base that
overlaps a different visible provider, such as a selectable child facade or one
of its routes, a local source or a registered package prefix, is a concrete
collision rejected eagerly with `MOTH-IMPORT-0006` even when no consumer names
it. Other ambiguity remains a use-site failure, not an eager publication
failure.

A private directory inside the exporting module may share a route spelling. It
stays private. `@child/forms suffix` finds only a `suffix` exported under the
route, never a file in `child/forms/`. An undeclared route or leaf reports an
import diagnostic instead of falling through to a private file.

A route is visible exactly where its facade is visible. That means from the
parent of a normal child module, from consumers allowed to use a scoped support
package, and from anywhere a registered source package is registered. Its names
sit beneath the facade path or registered package path.

The `.mtf` prelude stays root-only. Only facade-root constants from
`@core/html` and `@core/css` enter it. A routed export never adds a prelude
name, and `@core/html as html` does not enable `html.tables.cells`.

### Re-exporting dependencies

A facade may publish selected declarations defined in another file without moving them into the facade:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $export
    @components/card CardData as Card, render_card

    $export
    @core/math PI, sin
]

One `$export` publishes every selected name in its dependency clause. The alias
`Card` is the public name. `CardData` is not also published unless selected
separately. A route operand publishes the selection under that route instead of
the facade root. Bare namespaces, namespace aliases and wildcard selections
cannot be re-exported.

An ordinary dependency clause without `$export` binds names for the facade's own use but does not publish them. See @../packages/#public-reexports (Public re-exports) for selection and alias rules.

### Visibility boundaries

Files in one module can bind shared helper declarations through ordinary dependencies. Declarations authored directly in any root file remain local to that root. Other roots and helper files cannot read them, even if the roots share a directory.

Consumers in another module can bind only the applicable facade's explicitly
exported names, including names published under a public route. A normal module
publishes through `@mod.moth`. A scoped support or external project package
publishes through `@package.moth`. Route visibility follows facade visibility.
Bare `@child` exposes root members only, while `@child/forms` reaches the route
surface. Resolution never falls back between facades, to a private file or to a
physical directory that shares a route spelling.

See @#module-roots (Module roots) for file roles and @../packages/#module-visibility (Module visibility) for the complete access rules.

### Public API contracts

An exported signature must not expose private types, inaccessible trait requirements or project-private context. Exported functions and public receiver methods also need closed error contracts: implicit built-in failures must be handled or delivered through an explicit built-in `Error!` result. A custom error result needs local recovery or explicit mapping. The same rules apply to re-exports and routed publications.

See @../functions/#returns-and-multiple-values (Returns and multiple values) and @../errors/#propagation (Error propagation) for error handling.

